                         THREADS AND LOCKS
                         -----------------

The game uses the C++ standard library for threads and locks. The
types are in src/core/thread.

                              THREADS
                              -------

- main: the SDL events, the rendering of the draw command queue
  (TFB_FlushGraphics) and the utility keys. UQMGame::run.
- Starcon2Main: all the game logic. UQMGame starts it and joins it
  when it exits.
- audio stream decoder: decodes the music and speech streams
  (libs/sound/stream.cpp).
- nosound audio playback: only with the nosound driver.
- The SDL audio callback and OpenAL have their own threads. They use
  the mixer locks.

uqm::makeThread(name, func) starts a std::jthread. It sets the name
that debuggers show, and calls func(stopToken). A thread function
stops when stopToken.stop_requested() is true. The std::jthread
destructor requests a stop and joins the thread, so a thread is
stopped when its owner resets it (thread = {}).

                               LOCKS
                               -----

Use uqm::Mutex, or uqm::RecursiveMutex when a thread can lock the same
mutex again, and uqm::LockGuard to hold a lock for a scope. These wrap
std::mutex and std::recursive_mutex.

Clang builds use -Wthread-safety. Annotate the data that a lock
protects, and the functions that need a lock, with the macros in
core/thread/ThreadSafety.h:

    static uqm::Mutex s_mapLock {};
    static TFB_ColorMap* colormaps[MAX_COLORMAPS] UQM_GUARDED_BY(s_mapLock);

    static TFB_ColorMap* get_colormap(int index) UQM_REQUIRES(s_mapLock);

The analysis then reports a use of the data without the lock, and a
function that does not release a lock that it acquired. Other
compilers ignore the macros. The analysis cannot check std::mutex in
the MSVC library, so do not use std::mutex directly.

Use UQM_NO_THREAD_SAFETY_ANALYSIS only for a function that holds a
lock across function calls, for example Lock_DCQ and Unlock_DCQ.

Use std::condition_variable or std::condition_variable_any, and always
wait with a predicate. Use std::binary_semaphore for a one-time signal
between threads, as TFB_DrawScreen_WaitForSignal does.

Use std::atomic, not volatile, for a flag that more than one thread
reads or writes.

                          SHARED GAME DATA
                          ----------------

- Input: the main thread changes ImmediateInputState through the
  VControl bindings, and the game thread copies and resets it. Its
  values are std::atomic<int>, and VControl changes them with a
  compare-exchange loop. A recursive mutex in vcontrol.cpp guards the
  binding tables. Each public VControl function locks it.
- Draw command queue: the DCQ mutex guards the queue. Lock_DCQ and
  Unlock_DCQ hold it across calls.
- Sound: each TFB_SoundSource has a stream_mutex for its stream data.
  The stream functions in stream.h require it. The mixer locks
  src_mutex, buf_mutex and act_mutex, in that order.

                              SLEEPING
                              --------

libs/time/timelib.h has the game clock (GetTimeCounter in game ticks,
GetTimeMs in milliseconds, both from one start time) and sleep
functions that count in game ticks:

- SleepThread and SleepThreadUntil process the asynchronous operations
  (libs/callback/async.h) while they wait. Use them on the Starcon2Main thread.
- HibernateThread and HibernateThreadUntil only sleep.
- TaskSwitch sleeps for about 1 ms. Busy-wait loops call it so that
  they do not use all of a CPU core.
